    "body": "In the Unix world, `unlink` can delete open files; OS/2 forbids this. It's a long standing difference that constantly causes a lot of problems in the ported software. The latest case is working with Git repositories over SSH. VS Code tends to keep `.git/index` and `.git/refs/remote/*` open which makes the SSH daemon to keep them open on OS/2. Any request to `unlink` such a file (e.g. to update it via renaming a new version) will fail badly because the file is locked in the OS/2 kernel.\n\nWe used to have a tiny library by @YuriDario (`urpo` or such) that would postpone file deletion until process termination. But this solution is generally unreliable - it has too many edge cases where it would not work (e.g. other processes trying to do something with the file not being aware that it's deleted, and even within the same process opening this file once again would succeed although it should not, etc). So we dropped it completely - to force to fix in-process `unlink` in the ported software the clean way by making sure the file is closed before deletion. But this approach has its natural limits when a few processes are involved and the described Git case is one of them.\n\nI think I came up with an ultimate solution that will make `unlink` work once and for all. The idea is the following. Since we only care about kLIBC processes, the kLIBC DLL actually owns all file descriptors to the file being unlinked in all of them. So what `unlink` should do is temporarily close all open file descriptors to that file, both in the current process and in all other running kLIBC processes, then rename It to a temporary path (so that it could still be readable/writable under the existing FDs) and reopen this temporary path under the same FDs in all processes again. Then, the last process that closes such a temporary, will automatically delete it.\n\nkLIBC already has SPM for inter-process communication, so it should be doable. But it's not quick hack, so I will postpone it for now.",
